            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance rover news is that NASA says the rover has found evidence that a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter stack of ancient rock on the rim of Jezero Crater was formed by repeated asteroid impacts. The site, nicknamed the “Broom Point member,” contains layered bedrock that may be more than 3.9 billion years old, giving scientists a record of violent early-Martian impact history【6:0†source】.\n\nNASA also released new Broom Point-related imagery and route context on July 15, including a sweeping Mastcam-Z view looking back toward Jezero Crater’s rim and a map of Perseverance’s trip to the site【6:0†source】.\n\nAnother recent milestone: Perseverance has now driven the equivalent of a full marathon on Mars — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km — reaching that mark on sol 1,890 after about five years and four months of driving. That was much faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to cover the same distance【6:1†source】.",
